/* ============================================================================
   logarithmic-regression.epher -- fit y = a + b * ln(x) to a saturating curve

   Fits the least-squares logarithmic curve y = a + b * ln(x) to the
   response scores in `ys` measured at the doses in `xs` with the builtin
   logreg (guide 1.20), which prints the fitted model and the correlation
   r of the fit as a display string. A response that rises quickly and
   then flattens is the classic logarithmic shape, so doubling the dose
   adds a roughly constant amount of response. Data are illustrative.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/regression/logarithmic-regression.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\regression\logarithmic-regression.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/regression/logarithmic-regression.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The knobs to edit are `xs` and `ys` below. Everything above the results
   section is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const xs = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}   // dose, milligrams, doubling each step
const ys = {1.2, 1.7, 2.6, 3.1, 4.0, 4.5}   // response score, 0 to 5

// ---- the fitted logarithmic curve ---------------------------------------------------------
logreg(xs, ys)      // least-squares log fit, printed with its r

/* ---- expected output ----
= {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}
= {1.2, 1.7, 2.6, 3.1, 4, 4.5}
= y = 1.1429 + 0.9852*ln(x) (r = 0.9967)
*/